The PLY parser uses `getNextLine` with a 1MB cache. Let me check if `getNextLine` has the same bug.

Looking at the code again (lines 304-340):
```cpp
bool IOStreamBuffer<T>::getNextLine(std::vector<T> &buffer) {
    buffer.resize(m_cacheSize);  // line 305
    ...
    size_t i(0);
    while (!IsLineEnd(m_cache[m_cachePos])) {
        buffer[i] = m_cache[m_cachePos];  // line 327
        ++m_cachePos;
        ++i;
        if (m_cachePos >= m_cacheSize) {  // line 330
            if (!readNextBlock()) {  // line 331
                return false;
            }
        }
    }
    buffer[i] = '\n';  // line 336
    ++m_cachePos;
    return true;
}
```

Yes! Same bug in `getNextLine`. The buffer is resized to `m_cacheSize` at line 305, but `i` keeps growing when the line spans multiple blocks. And the PLY parser uses a 1MB cache!

So I can create a PLY file with a single line > 1MB (but < 10MB for the submission limit) that triggers the overflow.

Let me check what the PLY parser expects.

